    name: "Facebook \"Friend Request\" Digital Gold Trading App Scam",
    slug: "myanmar-facebook-digital-gold-trading-scam",
    description:
      "Myanmar's state-run Global New Light of Myanmar reported on February 20, 2024 that a wave of online gold-trading fraud had broken out across the country, with scammers specifically targeting housewives through a slow-building relationship scam that only later reveals itself as investment fraud. In the pattern described by victims interviewed for the report, the scheme starts when the victim accepts a Facebook friend request from a stranger who chats amiably over Messenger for a period of time to build rapport before steering the conversation toward business — namely, the stranger's supposed success trading gold online through a platform carrying the name of what looks like a legitimate, official Thailand-based investment company, complete with a fabricated website and mobile app built specifically to impersonate it. The scammer reinforces the friendship daily with reports of fresh profits and personal assurances that the victim's money is safe and cannot lose value, encouraging her to deposit progressively larger sums into the fake platform. When the victim eventually tries to withdraw her funds, or the full balance the app shows as her profit, she is told she must first pay an additional deposit — roughly a quarter of her original investment — before a withdrawal can be processed; victims who pay this second fee still find themselves unable to withdraw anything; and continuing to \"trade\" on the platform only produces further losses. The scheme surfaced as Myanmar's military-run government has separately moved against gambling and fraud networks operating in the country, and it mirrors the broader \"pig-butchering\" model used against victims worldwide, in which an engineered social-media friendship is used to manufacture trust before an investment con is introduced. Consumers are advised to treat any new online acquaintance who transitions from friendly chat into promoting a specific trading platform or app as a probable scam, to independently verify — through the real company's own official channels, not links or screenshots supplied by the new \"friend\" — that any investment platform bearing a well-known company's name is actually operated by that company, and to treat a demand for an additional payment before a withdrawal can be processed as confirmation that the platform is fraudulent rather than a normal fee.",
    categorySlug: "investment-fraud",
    alertLevel: "high",
    sources: ["Global New Light of Myanmar"],
    sourceUrl: "https://www.gnlm.com.mm/digital-gold-investment-scams-target-housewives/",
    country: "MM",
