    description: `Criminal gangs in Honduras contact technicians, tradespeople, and other service providers through social media or messaging apps while posing as an ordinary customer requesting a job, then lure the victim to a specific meeting point to supposedly perform the work. Once the victim arrives, the criminals launch a video call and threaten them, falsely claiming the area is under surveillance by a criminal organization and warning "la zona en la que se encuentra está siendo vigilada, así que no debe moverse" ("the area you're in is being watched, so you must not move") while gathering personal and family details to pressure the victim's relatives into transferring money by phone before the victim is allowed to leave. Honduras's Dirección Policial de Investigaciones (DPI) reported that this "false service" modality had produced 11 registered virtual-kidnapping cases nationwide by mid-2026, part of a broader wave of virtual-kidnapping and self-kidnapping schemes police have tracked through the year. The National Police advise anyone offering services to verify a prospective client's name, address, and phone number in advance, decline requests from suspicious or foreign numbers, insist on meeting in public, high-traffic locations, and collect a deposit up front, and to report any such incident immediately to the 24-hour Unidad Nacional Antisecuestros (UNAS) hotline at 9462-8612.`,
